I was hanging out with the cool kids in rec.games.roguelike.nethack when 
rempel@gmail.com got out a spraycan and scrawled the following:
>  Okay, so now I know that 2.2 and 2.3 are sucky versions. I have
>  checked and 3.1.3 is the first version where you don't lose the
>  spellbook after reading it.

You don't lose the spellbook after *successfully* reading it.  If you
fail to read it, one of the bad effects that can happen is that the book
disappears.

> I have tried the newer versions
>  occasionally, but they just lack the nostalgia factor, and they are
>  too complicated. I don't want to have to worry about alignments and
>  luck and deities and blah blah blah - just give me a nice sharp Katana
>  and a fireball spell and a room full of Orcs, not (gasp) multi-colored
>  monsters.

You can turn it back to monochrome if you prefer ;-)

>  Honestly I think one of the things that really kills the joy for me in
>  the new versions is having to kick in doors all the time. If they'd
>  just get rid of that bloody "The Door resists!" with all the other
>  things going on, why do I have to try opening a door multiple times?

Every time you get the "resists" message, you exercise your strength.
With maxed strength, the doors seem to resist a lot less of the time.

I've also found that kicking doors is a good way for clumsy characters
to exercise their Dexterity in those first 600 turns.

>  And to top it off they put an exclamation mark on it, as if this is
>  headline news. "This old dungeon door is a little stuck!!!! OMG! Wait,
>  let me write this in my diary!"

I found the exclamation marks a bit wearing, too.  But soon I stopped
noticing them.

>  Here's how I think it should work: doors should have three states:
>  open, closed and locked. If a door is open, anything can move through
>  it. If it is closed, intelligent creatures can move through it but it
>  is assumed that they are all polite and close it after themselves.
>  [...]

They're usually coming through the door with the express purpose of
attacking you.  They're not going to stop to close it while you're
beating a hasty retreat out of the other side of the room.

It would be a nice touch if wandering peacefuls closed any doors they
opened, but probably very hard to implement.

>  Oh - another pet peeve - Samurai is my favourite character class but
>  suddenly I have to figure out what a gunyoki is and a wakazushi and a
>  shikizumi and a kitinabe - WTF? is there an option to keep the regular
>  names for things?

The \ (show discoveries) command will show you the description of each
wieldable/wearable object.  That just leaves gunyoki (food), osaku
(lockpick), sake (booze) and koto (harp).

>  And newts and grid bugs? give me a frickin' break. Orcs, goblins and
>  zombies, those are real dungeon monsters. Seriously, who fights a
>  newt? Wouldn't you long sword get dull whacking at the ground trying
>  to hit it?

YANI: Large, booted creatures have a chance of stepping on tiny
creatures and killing them instantly.  This would be considered
dishonourable for lawfuls.  And if you're clumsy, you might accidentally
do this while trying to move into a peaceful (like with Stormbringer).

